   "text": "There once was a poet who loved to write limericks,  \nBut he found them hard to craft with a steady hand.  \nEach line had to be short and precise,  \nWith a twist at the end that would make you grin.  \nHe spent hours on each one, yet still it was hard.  \n\n***  \n\nThe first line was simple, the second a bit more,  \nThe third had a rhyme that was a bit too much.  \nThe fourth line was a bit too long,  \nAnd the fifth line was a bit too much.  \nHe tried again, and again, and again.  \n\n***  \n\nHe asked friends and family for help,  \nThey all said it was too hard to keep up.  \nHe tried different words and different rhymes,  \nBut the rhythm still didn’t seem to fit.  \nHe kept trying, but the limerick still felt off.  \n\n***  \n\nOne day, he found a book with some examples,  \nAnd from there he learned the way to make it right.  \nHe practiced every day, and slowly,  \nThe limerick began to take shape.  \nNow he writes them with ease and joy.  \n\n***  \n\nNow he writes limericks for friends and for foes,  \nEach one a little different, yet still true.  \nHe loves the sound of the rhythm, the flow,  \nThe twist at the end that makes you smile.  \nHe knows the rules, and he follows them well.",
